  "summary": "Large, fortified courtyard residences built by wealthy Shanxi merchants with defensive features.",
  "description": "Massive courtyard complexes with high walls, fortress-like structures, and intricate brick carvings reflecting the prosperity of Shanxi merchants. Developed during Ming and Qing dynasties as both residences and business headquarters, incorporating defensive elements due to regional instability.",
  "culturalContext": "Shanxi merchant compounds — the Qiao Family Courtyard, Wang Family Compound, Rishengchang's Pingyao bank headquarters — housed the Qing \"Jin merchants\" whose remittance banks (piaohao) financed imperial China: multi-generation fortress-courtyards of grey brick, carved brick-and-wood ornament, defensive watchtowers, and axial family hierarchy (\"three cardinal guides and five constant virtues\" in architecture). Pingyao's walled city is UNESCO-listed; the compounds anchor Chinese merchant-heritage tourism.",
